From 33936dfb7e2ae5d639b89bae44a17fda35d9e720 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Campos Ramos <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 11:55:01 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] scene: Keep popups off the edges of an output that shows
 stereo 3D

A popped thing that touches the edge of its output is flattened to the
screen's level, because something in front of the screen cut by the screen's
edge looks wrong. Instead of leaving the gap to the person, the system makes
it: an invisible border (kwinrc [StereoDepth] PopupBorder, default 10 logical
pixels, 0 switches it off, a slider in the stereo depth settings) shrinks the
area popups are placed in, on the sides that are the output's own edge (a
side next to a panel keeps the panel's edge).

- xdg_popup (menus, sub-menus, tooltips, select lists): the positioner's
  slide, flip and resize run against the shrunk area; an axis the popup
  cannot fit in even there keeps stock placement.
- the panel shell's popups placed by position (notifications, on-screen
  displays, tooltips, applet popups, and xdg_popups with a position set): moved
  inside, again when their real size arrives.
- override-redirect X11 popups (popup, dropdown, combo, tooltip,
  notification menus): moved inside once, as they are tracked.

Outputs that do not show 3D are unchanged. StereoDepth::popupBorder() is the
one place that decides which outputs the border applies to.

